1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,970
This is part two. If you haven't
seen part one, it's linked below.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,610
Here's the short version anyway.

3
00:00:07,453 --> 00:00:10,013
In two thousand and one
Microsoft released Internet

4
00:00:10,093 --> 00:00:13,643
Explorer six, and it
arrived with Windows XP.

5
00:00:14,593 --> 00:00:19,163
Within a couple of years it had more than
ninety percent of the browser market.

6
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:23,797
Microsoft used that to turn the
browser into an application platform.

7
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:26,097
It was a real idea.

8
00:00:26,747 --> 00:00:29,197
Web developers just
happened to pay for it.

9
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,990
That was part one. But
I left something out.

10
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,950
I left out the part
where the bugs had names.

11
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:39,543
Normally a browser bug
is a number in a tracker.

12
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:42,313
Issue four one seven two.

13
00:00:42,393 --> 00:00:43,963
Nobody remembers it.

14
00:00:44,393 --> 00:00:46,223
Nobody talks about it at lunch.

15
00:00:47,083 --> 00:00:50,383
In Internet Explorer six
the bugs had proper nouns.

16
00:00:50,463 --> 00:00:54,453
And that turns out to be the most
telling thing about the whole era.

17
00:00:57,223 --> 00:00:58,833
Start with a gentle one.

18
00:00:59,563 --> 00:01:02,713
You float an image to the left
and put a paragraph beside it.

19
00:01:03,303 --> 00:01:05,553
The text should sit
right next to the image.

20
00:01:06,283 --> 00:01:08,433
Instead it sits three pixels away.

21
00:01:09,229 --> 00:01:10,429
Not four.

22
00:01:10,609 --> 00:01:11,809
Not two.

23
00:01:11,943 --> 00:01:13,143
Three.

24
00:01:13,305 --> 00:01:15,035
Every time. It had a name.

25
00:01:15,485 --> 00:01:16,875
The Three Pixel Jog.

26
00:01:17,755 --> 00:01:21,485
Now float that image left and give
it a left margin of ten pixels.

27
00:01:22,555 --> 00:01:25,025
Internet Explorer six gives it twenty.

28
00:01:25,935 --> 00:01:29,665
It doubles the margin, but only
on the side you floated it to.

29
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:31,418
The Double Margin Bug.

30
00:01:31,498 --> 00:01:35,628
And the accepted fix was to
declare a floated element inline.

31
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:39,455
Which is nonsense. A float is a block.

32
00:01:40,325 --> 00:01:42,165
Calling it inline means nothing at all.

33
00:01:42,245 --> 00:01:43,975
It worked, so people wrote it.

34
00:01:44,654 --> 00:01:46,004
This next one is better.

35
00:01:46,754 --> 00:01:51,434
You load a page, and part of the content
simply isn't there. You scroll down.

36
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:54,044
You scroll back up. And it appears.

37
00:01:55,017 --> 00:01:57,927
Nothing loaded. Nothing
changed. It was just shy.

38
00:01:58,477 --> 00:02:01,667
And it had the right
name. The Peekaboo Bug.

39
00:02:02,499 --> 00:02:06,349
The remedy was to tell the missing
element that its position was relative.

40
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:10,149
Nobody could fully explain
why that brought it back.

41
00:02:10,938 --> 00:02:13,618
Then there is the one where
you hover over a link, and the

42
00:02:13,698 --> 00:02:17,188
background of the box below it
gets cut off in a straight line.

43
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:19,338
The Guillotine Bug.

44
00:02:20,103 --> 00:02:23,073
There was a bug where text crept
away from where you put it.

45
00:02:23,643 --> 00:02:25,093
Magic Creeping Text.

46
00:02:25,883 --> 00:02:27,583
There was one where
borders came out in the

47
00:02:27,663 --> 00:02:31,953
wrong place, in the wrong colour,
or not at all. Border Chaos.

48
00:02:32,791 --> 00:02:34,181
My favourite is quieter.

49
00:02:35,231 --> 00:02:37,151
You lay out a row of
boxes, and letters from

50
00:02:37,231 --> 00:02:41,961
the last one turn up again underneath
it. Not always. Not predictably.

51
00:02:43,051 --> 00:02:44,781
The Duplicate Character Bug.

52
00:02:45,587 --> 00:02:48,867
And what decided whether it happened
to you, and how many letters came

53
00:02:48,947 --> 00:02:53,377
out doubled, was the number of HTML
comments you had left in the markup.

54
00:02:54,407 --> 00:02:59,257
Comments. The one thing in the file
the browser is supposed to throw away.

55
00:03:00,465 --> 00:03:04,275
There is one more, and it's different,
because people used it on purpose.

56
00:03:05,165 --> 00:03:08,595
If you set a height and the content
is taller, the box should overflow.

57
00:03:09,365 --> 00:03:12,315
Internet Explorer six
grew the box instead.

58
00:03:13,216 --> 00:03:17,486
Which is wrong. It is also exactly
what a minimum height does.

59
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:21,226
And Internet Explorer six
had no minimum height.

60
00:03:21,476 --> 00:03:25,526
So developers stopped reporting that
one and started depending on it.

61
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:31,306
Stop and look at that
list. The Three Pixel Jog.

62
00:03:31,386 --> 00:03:34,746
The Double Margin Bug.
Peekaboo. The Guillotine.

63
00:03:34,826 --> 00:03:36,396
You don't name a bug you meet once.

64
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:39,597
These had names because everyone met them.

65
00:03:40,287 --> 00:03:42,357
And here is the part that makes it worse.

66
00:03:43,287 --> 00:03:45,397
Most of them had the
same cause underneath.

67
00:03:48,287 --> 00:03:51,527
Inside Internet Explorer
six, every element either

68
00:03:51,607 --> 00:03:54,077
did or did not have
something called layout.

69
00:03:54,975 --> 00:03:57,035
It decided whether the element sized and

70
00:03:57,115 --> 00:03:59,965
positioned itself, or
left that to its parent.

71
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:02,285
And you could not set it.

72
00:04:02,967 --> 00:04:06,477
There was no property called
layout, and no way to ask for it.

73
00:04:07,247 --> 00:04:08,977
It wasn't in any specification.

74
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:12,737
It was an internal flag
in the rendering engine.

75
00:04:13,541 --> 00:04:14,761
But you could flip it by accident.

76
00:04:14,841 --> 00:04:17,401
Setting a width did it.

77
00:04:17,481 --> 00:04:19,261
Setting a height did it.

78
00:04:19,341 --> 00:04:21,731
Floating an element did it.

79
00:04:22,501 --> 00:04:24,261
So half the fixes in this video were

80
00:04:24,341 --> 00:04:26,511
people flipping a switch
they couldn't see.

81
00:04:27,256 --> 00:04:30,826
Which is why they went looking for the
cheapest way to flip it on purpose.

82
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:33,766
One was to set a height of one percent.

83
00:04:34,757 --> 00:04:37,417
Every other browser had
to be kept away from that,

84
00:04:37,497 --> 00:04:40,367
so it went inside a hack
we'll get to in a minute.

85
00:04:41,167 --> 00:04:44,267
And in Internet Explorer
six it did no harm, because

86
00:04:44,347 --> 00:04:47,237
a height it can't fit is
one it grows past anyway.

87
00:04:48,367 --> 00:04:51,297
The bug from two minutes ago
is what made the fix safe.

88
00:04:52,103 --> 00:04:53,873
It had a name. The Holly Hack.

89
00:04:54,801 --> 00:04:58,571
The other way was a Microsoft
property called zoom, set to one.

90
00:04:59,141 --> 00:05:01,391
Zoom of one means do not zoom.

91
00:05:02,195 --> 00:05:05,745
It changed nothing you could
see. That was the point.

92
00:05:06,515 --> 00:05:11,145
It sat in stylesheets purely to set an
invisible flag that made the bugs go away.

93
00:05:14,245 --> 00:05:17,895
Which brings us to the hacks, and the
hacks are where this gets strange.

94
00:05:18,985 --> 00:05:22,235
To fix one browser you first have
to talk to only that browser.

95
00:05:23,197 --> 00:05:25,537
And Internet Explorer
six behaved as though

96
00:05:25,617 --> 00:05:28,727
there was another element
above the html element.

97
00:05:29,547 --> 00:05:31,127
No other browser did.

98
00:05:31,377 --> 00:05:32,827
It wasn't documented.

99
00:05:33,817 --> 00:05:35,327
It was simply how it acted.

100
00:05:36,227 --> 00:05:37,627
So you wrote a rule that matched the

101
00:05:37,707 --> 00:05:40,297
phantom, and only that
browser could see it.

102
00:05:41,147 --> 00:05:42,957
Star, space, html.

103
00:05:43,877 --> 00:05:47,967
Every other browser looked at it, found
nothing that matched, and moved on.

104
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:53,807
That is the star hack, and for years it
sat in almost every stylesheet on the web.

105
00:05:54,577 --> 00:05:56,607
There was also the underscore hack.

106
00:05:56,997 --> 00:05:59,847
You put an underscore in
front of a property name.

107
00:06:00,649 --> 00:06:04,419
A standards compliant browser
throws the line away as invalid.

108
00:06:05,449 --> 00:06:09,019
Internet Explorer six ignores
the underscore and applies it.

109
00:06:09,685 --> 00:06:12,225
So you deliberately broke
your own stylesheet,

110
00:06:12,405 --> 00:06:14,255
and the breakage was the instruction.

111
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:18,693
And then there is the one
Microsoft built on purpose.

112
00:06:19,403 --> 00:06:22,933
You could write a comment that
only Internet Explorer obeyed.

113
00:06:23,706 --> 00:06:28,336
To every other browser it was an ordinary
comment, ignored the way comments are.

114
00:06:29,366 --> 00:06:33,396
To Internet Explorer six it was a door
into a second version of the page.

115
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:37,757
Which means the vendor looked at the
situation, agreed that this browser

116
00:06:37,837 --> 00:06:42,127
needed a stylesheet of its own, and
shipped a feature to make that easier.

117
00:06:45,507 --> 00:06:47,837
Some of the damage
didn't need a bug at all.

118
00:06:48,947 --> 00:06:51,077
Internet Explorer six had two modes.

119
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:53,737
In the modern one it
followed the standard.

120
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:57,350
In the old one, the width of
a box included its padding

121
00:06:57,430 --> 00:07:00,700
and its border, which is a
completely different number.

122
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:04,663
Which mode you got depended on
the first line of the document.

123
00:07:05,333 --> 00:07:08,813
And the version of HTML
that everyone was moving

124
00:07:08,893 --> 00:07:12,043
to told you to put an
XML declaration up there.

125
00:07:12,681 --> 00:07:16,111
That line pushed Internet
Explorer six into the old mode.

126
00:07:16,861 --> 00:07:19,251
Following the newer
standard was what broke it.

127
00:07:21,771 --> 00:07:23,501
Then there is transparency.

128
00:07:24,531 --> 00:07:27,391
Internet Explorer six couldn't
show a PNG with soft edges.

129
00:07:27,471 --> 00:07:30,761
Where the image should fade, it put grey.

130
00:07:31,578 --> 00:07:36,748
Microsoft's answer was a filter with a
name like a serial number. It worked.

131
00:07:37,628 --> 00:07:40,918
But it painted outside the
browser's normal rendering process,

132
00:07:41,208 --> 00:07:44,258
so links you put on top of
it would often stop working.

133
00:07:45,228 --> 00:07:47,438
You fixed the picture and broke the link.

134
00:07:48,179 --> 00:07:51,259
And fading anything else was
a second filter, with its

135
00:07:51,339 --> 00:07:54,719
own syntax, sitting in the
stylesheet right next to the

136
00:07:54,799 --> 00:07:57,769
ordinary property that every
other browser understood.

137
00:07:59,329 --> 00:08:01,639
Some of this predates the bugs entirely.

138
00:08:02,429 --> 00:08:05,839
Before any of it, layouts were
built out of nested tables.

139
00:08:06,701 --> 00:08:09,721
And to hold a gap open you used
a transparent image one pixel

140
00:08:09,801 --> 00:08:15,351
across, stretched to whatever size the
gap needed to be. It had a name too.

141
00:08:16,001 --> 00:08:17,201
The spacer gif.

142
00:08:19,471 --> 00:08:21,041
Hovering was another one.

143
00:08:21,611 --> 00:08:24,281
In Internet Explorer six,
hover worked on links.

144
00:08:24,411 --> 00:08:25,961
Only on links.

145
00:08:26,803 --> 00:08:29,723
So a menu that opened when you
moved the mouse over anything

146
00:08:29,803 --> 00:08:33,313
else needed a small program
attached to the stylesheet.

147
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:36,091
Not a script in the page.

148
00:08:37,021 --> 00:08:40,161
A separate file, referenced
from CSS, holding code

149
00:08:40,241 --> 00:08:43,131
whose job was to teach
the browser one selector.

150
00:08:44,041 --> 00:08:46,471
People passed that file around for years.

151
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:51,280
Part one mentioned that you could
put JavaScript inside a stylesheet.

152
00:08:52,050 --> 00:08:55,320
What it didn't mention is how
often that JavaScript ran.

153
00:08:56,089 --> 00:08:59,639
Every mouse move. Every
keystroke. Every scroll.

154
00:08:59,749 --> 00:09:02,739
Over and over again,
to work out one width.

155
00:09:04,107 --> 00:09:06,937
And the browser had ceilings
that nobody else had.

156
00:09:07,747 --> 00:09:11,497
A stylesheet stopped applying
somewhere past four thousand rules.

157
00:09:12,067 --> 00:09:15,917
Anything after the thirty first
stylesheet on a page was ignored.

158
00:09:16,793 --> 00:09:19,993
Which sounds like a number you
would never reach, until your site

159
00:09:20,073 --> 00:09:23,453
is large enough that somebody has
to write a build step whose only

160
00:09:23,533 --> 00:09:27,523
job is to merge stylesheets together
before the browser sees them.

161
00:09:30,193 --> 00:09:32,283
And some of it wasn't cosmetic at all.

162
00:09:33,153 --> 00:09:36,833
Internet Explorer six counted its
page objects and its script objects

163
00:09:36,913 --> 00:09:40,823
in two different ways, and neither
side could see the whole loop.

164
00:09:41,534 --> 00:09:44,654
So if a script pointed at part
of the page, and that part of

165
00:09:44,734 --> 00:09:48,164
the page pointed back at the
script, neither was ever released.

166
00:09:49,154 --> 00:09:52,664
Leave that window open long enough
and the browser ate the machine.

167
00:09:55,224 --> 00:09:57,474
But the best one isn't
a layout bug at all.

168
00:09:58,264 --> 00:10:00,694
You write an input tag.
You give it a type.

169
00:10:00,944 --> 00:10:03,094
And you forget the equals sign.

170
00:10:03,793 --> 00:10:05,643
That is a malformed attribute.

171
00:10:06,433 --> 00:10:11,633
Every parser ever written has an answer
for that. Ignore it. Guess at it.

172
00:10:11,713 --> 00:10:12,913
Carry on.

173
00:10:13,197 --> 00:10:15,047
Internet Explorer crashed.

174
00:10:15,717 --> 00:10:18,717
Inside, it wanted to know
whether the field was hidden,

175
00:10:18,797 --> 00:10:21,467
so it compared what you had
typed against the word hidden.

176
00:10:22,477 --> 00:10:23,807
You hadn't typed anything.

177
00:10:24,731 --> 00:10:27,921
So it went to fetch the thing you
hadn't typed from address zero.

178
00:10:28,031 --> 00:10:31,451
Which would be an ordinary
browser crash, except

179
00:10:31,531 --> 00:10:34,041
that the code doing it
was not in the browser.

180
00:10:34,963 --> 00:10:36,793
It was in a shared system library.

181
00:10:37,843 --> 00:10:39,863
So those three words
also took down the mail

182
00:10:39,943 --> 00:10:42,913
client, and the preview
pane in the file manager.

183
00:10:43,722 --> 00:10:47,612
A window listing files on your
own machine, closed by a web page.

184
00:10:48,382 --> 00:10:51,472
That is part one's argument
arriving as a crash report.

185
00:10:52,282 --> 00:10:54,952
The browser wasn't a program
running on the system.

186
00:10:55,742 --> 00:10:57,172
It was part of the system.

187
00:11:00,462 --> 00:11:02,932
And you couldn't simply
test the fix somewhere else.

188
00:11:03,502 --> 00:11:05,802
Internet Explorer was
welded to the operating

189
00:11:05,882 --> 00:11:09,612
system, so one computer
ran exactly one version.

190
00:11:12,452 --> 00:11:14,376
Which is why a front
end job in two thousand

191
00:11:14,456 --> 00:11:17,142
and six came with a
shelf of spare machines.

192
00:11:18,073 --> 00:11:21,713
And later with a folder of virtual
ones, each carrying a whole

193
00:11:21,793 --> 00:11:25,663
operating system so that one
page could be opened inside it.

194
00:11:26,297 --> 00:11:28,487
And then somebody did the obvious thing.

195
00:11:29,337 --> 00:11:33,387
If you can teach the browser one missing
feature, you can teach it all of them.

196
00:11:34,075 --> 00:11:37,495
A developer called Dean Edwards
wrote a library whose entire purpose

197
00:11:37,575 --> 00:11:42,005
was to make Internet Explorer six
behave like a later version of itself.

198
00:11:42,695 --> 00:11:45,725
People shipped that. In production.

199
00:11:48,365 --> 00:11:50,315
Which is where it stops being funny.

200
00:11:51,125 --> 00:11:53,665
For most of a decade, a
serious share of the world's

201
00:11:53,745 --> 00:11:59,135
front end work went into one browser's
mistakes. Not features. Not products.

202
00:11:59,965 --> 00:12:00,993
Compensation.

203
00:12:01,073 --> 00:12:03,943
And it lasted because of the
thing part one was about.

204
00:12:04,713 --> 00:12:08,553
Companies had built real applications
on it, the kind that only

205
00:12:08,633 --> 00:12:12,223
ran there, and those applications
still had to work on Monday.

206
00:12:13,139 --> 00:12:18,029
So the browser that everybody wanted gone
was the one nobody was allowed to remove.

207
00:12:18,867 --> 00:12:21,067
In two thousand and ten, Google announced

208
00:12:21,147 --> 00:12:23,547
that its own apps would
stop working in it.

209
00:12:23,627 --> 00:12:28,197
And a design studio in Denver
decided that deserved a funeral.

210
00:12:31,357 --> 00:12:34,587
An actual one. A coffin. A eulogy.

211
00:12:35,417 --> 00:12:38,907
Between eighty and a hundred people
came to watch a browser get buried.

212
00:12:39,637 --> 00:12:40,967
They had planned for thirty.

213
00:12:41,806 --> 00:12:43,496
And Microsoft sent flowers.

214
00:12:44,466 --> 00:12:47,956
The card said, thanks for the
good times, Internet Explorer six.

215
00:12:48,794 --> 00:12:52,244
One year later, to the day,
Microsoft launched a website.

216
00:12:53,114 --> 00:12:54,754
It tracked how much of the world was still

217
00:12:54,834 --> 00:12:58,284
running Internet Explorer
six, country by country.

218
00:12:59,005 --> 00:13:01,405
At that point it was still twelve percent.

219
00:13:01,485 --> 00:13:03,735
The goal was to push it under one.

220
00:13:04,405 --> 00:13:06,285
The company that built
the browser was running

221
00:13:06,365 --> 00:13:09,315
a public countdown to
its own product's death.

222
00:13:12,135 --> 00:13:14,065
So why did the bugs have names?

223
00:13:14,875 --> 00:13:17,105
Because you name the things
you cannot get around.

224
00:13:18,014 --> 00:13:21,084
Nobody names the weather in a
country they are passing through.

225
00:13:21,734 --> 00:13:23,124
You name it where you live.

226
00:13:23,814 --> 00:13:26,624
And for about ten years,
that was where the web lived.

227
00:13:29,754 --> 00:13:33,284
But getting Internet Explorer six
off the web was another story.

228
00:13:34,114 --> 00:13:35,764
And that is where we go next.

229
00:13:36,374 --> 00:13:40,904
If you want that one, there is a subscribe
button, and part one is linked below.
